Z||000000||Fiero Fiend||11-05-2001||12:25 AM||||Hello <BR>I was at a new car dealer today with my dad.  We were looking around the back of the lot when I came across an 84 Indy Fiero in good shape. Unfortunately it's in with the cars to be auctioned off. I am hoping they will let me make a deal on the car tomorrow but it's doubtful since they deal in new Fords and it's an old Pontiac. I have always been a Fiero fan since I was a kid and have always wanted one. I wanted a GT but after reading about the Indy I found out it is pretty rare. I wonder what a good offer would be to make on it. Could anyone give me any advice?  It's an 84 Indy Fiero.  Did that car come with a V6 or a 4 cyl.? <BR>Thanks for any help.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||11-05-2001||12:35 AM||||Welcome to the forum. Indys are pretty rare...thats a good find. Find out if they'll take an offer from you in leu of it being auctioned off. <BR>Pontiac only made somewhere around 2000 Indys (the exact # is still pretty much unknown, but right around 2000). There is also u/k #'s about how many were autos and how many were manny transmissions. Dont tell the dealer this (obviously), make him an offer, but dont seem too enthused. I guess if its in decent condition, Id start off at around 1,500 bucks...maybe 2k...youll need to feel him out (you know what I mean) first.<BR>Indys only came 4 cylinders. Good luck, and keep us posted!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My7logo_1.gif">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 11-05-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||Fiero Fiend||11-05-2001||12:46 AM||||Thanx for the advice!  This one is an automatic. It has 108,000 miles. The car isn't in the best of shape but it's not bad.  It has all the indy decals and badges as far as I know. It looks like a simple detail would do wonders. I probably wont sleep a wink tonight wondering what his answer will be! I recently bought a truck from their top salesman so hopefully that might influence him to deal with me. I'll let y'all know how it goes.<BR>Thanks!<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fiend (edited 11-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroMaster88||11-05-2001||02:53 AM||||It sounds like a nice car!  Go in and see what they say for a price.  Or just start out with a really low offer.  It never hurts to try.  A friend of mine got his 87 GT with 90K miles and in mint condition for $1000, the guy was asking $4000.  Good luck!  I hope you get it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                  <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Haze_Performance||11-05-2001||03:19 AM||||I agree with My7Fieros.. Start at $1000 to $1500 & go from there. I don't think the price would go to high at an auction, so they should try to work with you.. Good Luck & heres a little something to keep your keep your focus...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> GO GET EM!!!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSC000051.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSC000111.jpg"> <P>Good luck & Keep us updated....<BR>-Haze  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/reallogo.gif"> <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="red">Goto Website - <A HREF="http://www.ClassicFiero.com" TARGET=_blank>Click Here</A> </FONT></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000005||Fiero Fiend||11-05-2001||07:18 PM||||Bummer!<BR>I found out today that the car was already promised to a wholesaler. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I'm totally bummed out. Although it wasn't nearly as nice as Haze Performance's ride it looked exactly the same. I'm not giving up though, I'm determined to find a nice GT. Thanks for all the advice guys!||||reg||9
